pleased to say the General Secretary of  the TUC Paul Novak joins us now Paul  good morning to you thanks for being  with us this morning now you've talked  about a fair deal for workers the nurses  are asking for five percent above RPI  inflation is that a fair deal  considering the pay restraints that the  government must have in place well  listen we've got 133 000 vacancies in  our NHS at the moment we've got a  staffing crisis and I don't think  another real terms pay cut for nurses  but not just nurses for paramedics for  physiotherapists for anybody working on  our NHS is going to help solve that  staff Staffing crisis so I think what's  important now Kamal is that we've got  ministers who are empowered to talk  about pay with unions and to listen to  their own Workforce so yesterday I wrote  to the Prime Minister and asked him to  urgently meet with me and other union  leaders but crucially he's got allow his  ministers to get around a table with our  unions and reach a fair settlement on  pay what their Fair settlement is going  to look like I can't say and I can't  negotiate live on your program this  morning but we really do need to have  those negotiations you suggest that you  want it to be above inflation and  inflation's running at what 10.7 right  now yeah but as I say it's not my job to  negotiate the pay of our nurses or  paramedics or anybody else what I'm  saying is those workers very clearly and  I was on those picket lines with the  paramedics just before Christmas each  and every one of those workers told me  they can't afford another year of a real  terms pay cut the reality is their  mortgages are going up their food bills  are going up their rental mortgages are  going up the only thing that isn't going  up is their their wages and I think the  government has to take some  responsibility here now step up to the  plate facilitate those talks let's get a  fair pay settlement for our NHS workers  but indeed public sector workers right  across the piece and you've asked for a  meeting with the Prime Minister you've  sent the letter what today when you're  expecting a response and what do you  think he will say to you well I would  hope you recognize the urgency of a  meeting and just to say I mean during  the pandemic the TC work very closely  with Rishi sunak and developing the  fairlow scheme alongside the CBI it  seems that the Prime Minister has  forgotten some of those basic lessons  from the pandemic that actually we come  up with better solution when we work  together and you know the NHS Staffing  crisis isn't going to solve itself  neither is the Staffing crisis in other  parts of our Public Services one is six  new teachers leaves the profession  within a year of qualifying there is  something wrong with our public services  and that's what happens when you have a  decade of underfunding under Staffing  and real terms pay cut to a public  sector workers I mean you say he's  forgotten some of the principles what do  you think he's forgotten I think he's  forgotten the value of sitting down and  reaching an agreement and and taking  ideas from people outside his own  cabinets and frankly you know I think  it'd be really good for issue soon act  to talk to our unions but also to talk  to some of those Frontline paramedics  those physiotherapists the teachers in  the classrooms civil servants people who  for the last decade have been placed  under real pressures on workload who  feel at breaking points let the Prime  Minister talk to those workers and  understand their concerns and their  issues I think that's what any decent  employer would do and it's someone  responsible for the employment of  millions of public sector workers that's  exactly what the Prime Minister should  do as well and of course the government  will say that there are pay offers on  the table for nurses there are pay  offers on the table for some of the  other people who are out on strike so  this is not a case of them not listening  this is a case of of unions trying to  demand even more that's unaffordable at  this point it's not a case of unions  demand and the unaffordable politics is  all about choices and the government has  got some big Clear Choices in front of  us in front of it it could for example  decide to tax more heavily the excess  profits of the oil and gas companies it  could decide to tax wealth in the same  way taxes incomes but the reality is  each and every single one of our public  sector workers at the moment faces a  real terms pay cut and that comes on the  back of a decade of pay cuts the average  nurse four thousand pound a year worse  off than if their pay would have risen  by the rate of inflation since 2008  something has got to give and I think  the Prime Minister needs to listen to  his own Workforce let's talk a little  bit more about nursing things you've  brought them up again you say  something's got to give now as I said  they're asking for five percent above  RPI now when they asked for that back  back in the summer I think it was going  to run about 14 would be the payoff for  now as we know it's around about 19 do  you think that this might be a misstep  in their strategy and would you like to  see them come back  with a different demand listen the  Dynamics always change in a pay  negotiation once you're sat around a  table but what we've got at the city at  moments is for example Steve Barkley  just before Christmas our health  secretary in the midst of a dispute  around pay as said he'll talk to our  unions about anything other than pay I  just don't think that's tenable and the  only way you're going to pay review  boards you deal with that uh listen the  government has been using the  independent review buddies as a human  shield and I think is in real danger of  discrediting those bodies when those  bodies met and made their  recommendations we didn't have inflation  running at 10 11 the government in its  evidence those pay review bodies set the  very clear Financial parameters that the  bodies could work within so listen let's  not hide behind the pay review bodies  what we need is the government to sit  around the table with our unions and  reach an agreement I mean there are  serious calls that important some  accusations as well that perhaps the  government is not being entirely fair in  this negotiations now you want to have a  meeting with the Prime Minister about  this are you going to tell him that you  think he's sabotaging the negotiations  they're not being fair well absolutely I  mean I I'll say to the Prime Minister  directly what I'd say uh to you uh this  morning and you know the key thing for  me I'm not interested in political Point  scoring I'm not interested in meeting  the the Prime Minister just for you know  uh Tea and Sympathy or indeed beer and  sandwiches I'm interested in meeting the  Prime Minister our unions are interested  in meeting the prime minister so we can  resolve these disputes and put pay in  the pockets of hard work and public  service workers and deliver the public  services that the people of this country  deserve and the Public's verdict is  pretty clear two-thirds of the public  blame the government for mishandling  these strikes it's time for the  government to listen to the public  listen to its own Workforce get round  the table with unions and while many of  course will accept those uh calls by the  public sector workers  um there is a real hit to the economy by  some of these strikes not of course not  with nurses rights but with the train  driver strikes and the train worker  strikes some 2.5 billion pounds lost to  the economy I mean as the country's  saying that it can't afford to raise  wages it can't afford to deal with the  the loss to income that is getting from  the strike action well there's a real  hypocrisy here because every single day  of strike action while rail workers are  losing pay the government is effectively  underwriting the losses of the  privatized trained companies that makes  no Financial sense whatsoever and the  other thing that really sucks demand out  of local economies is when rail workers  or indeed nurses teachers Supermarket  workers don't have money in their  pockets to spend in local shops and  restaurants now are our rail unions are  very clear they want those disputes  resolved they want their members back to  work they regret any impact on the  economy but the responsibility for these  strikes the responsibility for the  strikes in the public sector lies very  clearly with the governments I mean that  regret is of course truly felt but these  strikes are going on I spoke to Michael  and Chester they said they could run on  for months to come he's got a mandate up  until May you'll know that many mandates  run until it well into the new year how  do you expect these strikes to continue  because the strike action so far has not  had anything like the effect you'd like  it to have on the government making  those decisions well I think it's had a  very clear impact on on public opinion  and I know I know talking to people who  walk past that picket line uh in  Waterloo a couple of weeks ago the  public are very clear when it comes to a  straight fight between Rishi sunak  Jeremy Hunt and Steve Barkley and our  nurses and paramedics I know where  public sympathy lies I would like the  government to listen to the concerns of  their own workforces forget me forget  union leaders think about the millions  of people day in Day Out Who deliver our  Public Services the least the government  could do is show them the respect of  listening to them talking to them and  reach an agreement if they don't you're  not in a general strike yet but there  are consecutive days of action it feels  like almost like a general strike is  that where you go to next how does this  how do you increase the pressure well  well you know it's a really difficult  decision for any worker who takes strike  action they do lose pay they don't get  to deliver the services that they're  proud to deliver so my responsibility  the responsibility of our unions is when  members take that difficult decision we  have to make sure that that is as  impactful as possible and as effective  as possible so we're always talking to  our unions about how we make sure we  maximize the impact of industrial action  how we maximize the call on the  government I you know I don't want to be  in a position least of all our public  sector workers don't want to be in a  position where people are taking strike  action but really the ball is in the  government's Court they need to talk to  their own Workforce we've seen employers  right across the private sector where  workers have taken industrial action  listen to their own workforces and reach  agreements and I dare to the government  to do the same so there can be more  coordination you want to see more  pressure being put forward well I think  it absolutely makes sense for example in  education and in health where you've got  a number of different unions for unions  to talk to each other about how we  support each other coordinate our action  that makes absolute sense it's clear on  what you're saying here so support your  other coordinary action that means going  out on the same day all out on strike no  it doesn't necessarily mean that at all  uh Kamali I mean it may actually be  better to to have a focus for  particularly groups of workers on  particular days and you know what what  what I'm really interested in is not  posturing or grand strategizing what I'm  really interested in is getting a  resolution for public sector workers and  you know I as I say I would urge the  prime minister to talk to our units and  talk to those Frontline workers because  I think they deserve the respect of  being listened to and their concerns  taken on board is there is there a  concern is there a danger in this action  and if it does increase in ways that  you're describing the the government try  and push through some more of those  anti-strike laws and I know that I'm  sure that will be something you  discussed with the Prime Minister as  well if you get that meeting I mean that  that is a real  um uh danger I think it would be a real  mistake from the government because what  that would be doing is treating the  symptoms of the strikes rather than the  underlying causes which is those real  terms uh pay cuts but let me be  absolutely clear if the Prime Minister  brings forward more legislation to make  it even more difficult for workers to  take strike action the TUC will fight  that will fight that politically we'll  fight it  um legally as well we already have a  legal challenge in with the government  11 TUC unions challenging the  government's decision to allow employers  to use agency workers to break strikes  we think the governments will be very  close to a breach in fundamental  International labor law and we'd ask  them to to step back from that Brink and  rather than messing around with the  right to strike what they should be  doing is negotiating settlement to these  disputes and delivering a fair pay rise  for public sector workers okay pull no  back from CCO do you appreciate your  time this morning thank you very much  for coming

